WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:06.160
Director Matt Reeves ditches a traditional
origin story for his latest movie, "The Batman."

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.820
Instead, the 2022 film begins with Bruce Wayne
well into his second year of vigilante work

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.530
in Gotham City.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:18.900
He's a brutal force on the streets as he breezes
through fights with thugs and seasoned gangsters

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.369
alike to protect the innocent — although
he's definitely still a little rough around

00:00:23.369 --> 00:00:24.369
the edges.

00:00:24.369 --> 00:00:28.929
Regular citizens aren't sure whether the Dark
Knight is a hero or a monster, and he still

00:00:28.929 --> 00:00:33.399
has an incredibly uneasy relationship with
the Gotham City Police Department despite

00:00:33.399 --> 00:00:36.899
the unquestioning trust Jim Gordon places
in him.

00:00:36.899 --> 00:00:41.980
Things in Gotham go from bad to worse when
the Riddler rears his ugly, mask-covered mug

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.989
to target institutional corruption in Gotham.

00:00:44.989 --> 00:00:46.859
He starts with the mayor.

00:00:46.859 --> 00:00:52.539
From there, Batman and Selina Kyle, aka Catwoman,
try to figure out why the Riddler is hell-bent

00:00:52.539 --> 00:00:54.629
on tearing the city down.

00:00:54.629 --> 00:01:00.159
Unfortunately, that isn't so easy when the
killer's mysterious game is built upon riddles,

00:01:00.159 --> 00:01:03.270
mind-bending mysteries, and a criminal conspiracy.

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:07.200
The early reception to the movie has been
incredibly positive, and as of this video

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:13.479
it currently has an 86% critic score on Rotten
Tomatoes, with WhatToWatch saying it, quote,

00:01:13.479 --> 00:01:14.990
"fires on all cylinders."

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:19.121
But, like every good comic book movie, "The
Batman" ends with a dramatic fight to save

00:01:19.121 --> 00:01:20.390
the day.

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:24.290
It's time to look at the hero's tactics in
the Gotham Arena brawl.

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:26.549
Careful, spoilers ahead!

00:01:26.549 --> 00:01:32.360
The Riddler's plan to destroy Gotham's infrastructure
culminates with the detonation of seven bombs

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:36.009
around the city's sea wall, flooding the downtown
area.

00:01:36.009 --> 00:01:40.540
Thousands of Gotham citizens congregating
downtown to celebrate Bella Real's mayoral

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:44.380
win rush to the Gotham Arena to escape the
high waters.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:49.700
As they seek safety, a gang of Riddler's followers,
all dressed in matching uniforms, open fire

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.250
from rigging high above the arena.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:56.560
Thankfully, Batman and Catwoman are on the
scene to take them down and save the people

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:01.859
down below, but the Caped Crusader might have
broken his rule against killing in the midst

00:02:01.859 --> 00:02:03.259
of fighting back.

00:02:03.259 --> 00:02:08.500
Historically, whether it's in film, TV, comics,
or video games, Batman always makes a point

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:10.530
that he doesn't kill criminals.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:15.020
This rule stems from the trauma of watching
his parents die that night in Crime Alley.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:20.030
However, during "The Batman" finale, as the
gunmen working for Riddler take shots at Batman

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:24.310
at close range, he deflects the bullets back
at the shooters, which sends a few of them

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:25.860
slumping to the floor.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.990
It's not clear if their wounds are fatal,
but it doesn't exactly look good.

00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:33.000
Obviously, there's the suspension of disbelief
here.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:38.080
The audience doesn't see Batman kill anyone,
nor do we see the Riddler's gunmen die in

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:39.290
the fight.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:43.890
Even the lone goon he nearly beats to a pulp
is saved when Jim Gordon intervenes.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:48.080
As such, it's easy to assume that Batman stuck
to his one rule.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:53.080
But, in this grim and gritty world that Matt
Reeves has created, it wouldn't be a stretch

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:56.980
to think that if they died because of the
fight, Batman wouldn't be too bothered by

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:57.980
it.

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:01.700
Because the film ends on a surprisingly hopeful
note, maybe the Bat will have a different

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:05.580
outlook when it comes to brutalizing his foes
from now on.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.880
In the last decade or so, the debate over
whether Batman inadvertently kills people

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:14.140
has become a hot-button topic — even hitting
a fever pitch with the Dark Knight's actions

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:17.240
in "Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice."

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:21.970
Zack Snyder's second movie in the DC Extended
Universe sees Ben Affleck's hero dish out

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:26.180
some brutal punishment to Lex Luthor's goons
across the film.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:30.990
Whether it's his chaotic car chase through
Gotham Harbor or that savage warehouse fight,

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:34.930
the older version of the Dark Knight doesn't
pull his punches at all.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:38.980
Realistically, it's all about the story that
Batman happens to be in at the time.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:44.520
The "no killing" rule can be pushed or broken
depending on which director or author is controlling

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:45.520
Batman's story.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:49.500
With the Robert Pattinson-led "The Batman,"
it's clear that this version of the hero is

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:54.020
still questioning his approach to fighting
crime; even he acknowledges he needs to be

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:56.530
a better symbol to the people of Gotham.

00:03:56.530 --> 00:04:01.470
So, although he's not too bothered by deflecting
bullets back at his foes, he is at least trying

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:02.620
to be better.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:06.910
Obviously, there's an argument to be made
that Bruce Wayne isn't actively trying to

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:11.860
kill the Riddler's followers while in Batman
mode; he's just defending himself.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:16.979
In the most idealized versions of the character,
like "Batman: The Animated Series," the specifics

00:04:16.979 --> 00:04:19.949
about his "no killing" rule definitely matter.

00:04:19.949 --> 00:04:25.009
But when the story is directly analyzing who
Batman is and how he's growing as a hero,

00:04:25.009 --> 00:04:29.039
there's a little bit of breathing room when
it comes to his violent streak.

